From: Switch-CrOS Authors
Subject: [PATCH] Drop ANGLE's Intel/NVIDIA OpenGL ES blocklist

ANGLE refuses to use NVIDIA's GLES driver via its DisplayGLX backend
(libANGLE/renderer/gl/glx/DisplayGLX.cpp).  The check is a workaround
for ANGLE's automated test infrastructure detecting Intel/NVIDIA cards
with desktop OpenGL backends rather than a real driver-compatibility
issue — the comment at the call site explicitly cites "automated
testing" rationale.

On Switch (Nintendo Switch / Tegra X1 running L4T 4.9), NVIDIA's GLES is
the only hardware-accelerated path: there is no DRM/KMS, no Mesa GLES
backend, and software rendering (SwiftShader) is unusably slow on this
CPU.  Skip the check so Chrome can use NVIDIA's GLES via ANGLE.

Upstream tracking: https://anglebug.com/42260314

--- a/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/renderer/gl/glx/DisplayGLX.cpp
+++ b/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/renderer/gl/glx/DisplayGLX.cpp
@@ -322,19 +322,12 @@ egl::Error DisplayGLX::createRenderer(glx::Context shareContext,
             return egl::Error(EGL_NOT_INITIALIZED, "Context lost for unknown reason.");
         }
     }
-    // TODO(cwallez, http://anglebug.com/42260314) Disable the OpenGL ES backend on Linux NVIDIA and
-    // Intel as it has problems on our automated testing. An OpenGL ES backend might not trigger
-    // this test if there is no Desktop OpenGL support, but that's not the case in our automated
-    // testing.
-    VendorID vendor = GetVendorID(functionsGL.get());
-    bool isOpenGLES =
-        eglAttributes.get(EGL_PLATFORM_ANGLE_TYPE_ANGLE, EGL_PLATFORM_ANGLE_TYPE_DEFAULT_ANGLE) ==
-        EGL_PLATFORM_ANGLE_TYPE_OPENGLES_ANGLE;
-    if (isOpenGLES && (IsIntel(vendor) || IsNvidia(vendor)))
-    {
-        return egl::Error(EGL_NOT_INITIALIZED,
-                          "Intel or NVIDIA OpenGL ES drivers are not supported.");
-    }
+    // switch-t210: ANGLE's upstream blocklist for Intel/NVIDIA GLES
+    // exists only to avoid confusing ANGLE's automated test infra (see
+    // anglebug.com/42260314).  NVIDIA's GLES is the only hardware-accel
+    // path available on Switch (no DRM/KMS, no Mesa Tegra backend,
+    // SwiftShader is unusably slow on Tegra X1's CPU), so the check is
+    // removed entirely here.

     syncXCommands(false);

